<!DOCTYPE html>
<html><head><meta charset="utf-8"/><meta content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1" name="viewport"/><title>Daily Multivendor Routing Lab — 2026-08-29-08-11</title><style>body{font-family:Arial,sans-serif;background:#eef2f6;color:#17212b;margin:0;line-height:1.55}main{max-width:1200px;margin:auto;padding:20px}.hero{background:#102a43;color:white;padding:28px;border-radius:14px}.card,.lesson,.q,.fig{background:white;border:1px solid #d5dee7;border-radius:12px;padding:18px;margin:12px 0;overflow-wrap:anywhere}h2{border-bottom:2px solid #d5dee7;padding-bottom:6px}a{color:#075f9f;overflow-wrap:anywhere}.badge{display:inline-block;border:1px solid #d5dee7;border-radius:999px;padding:2px 7px;font-size:12px}.grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(280px,1fr));gap:12px}.fig{text-align:center;background:#fff}.fig img{max-width:100%;height:auto;background:white;padding:10px}.fb{display:none;border:2px dashed #d5dee7;padding:24px}.q label{display:block;padding:4px}.q button,.controls button{background:#075f9f;color:white;border:0;border-radius:7px;padding:8px 11px;margin:5px}.feedback{display:none;background:#eef7ff;padding:9px;border-radius:7px}.good{background:#ebf8ef}.bad{background:#fff0f0}table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse}td,th{border:1px solid #d5dee7;padding:7px;text-align:left;vertical-align:top}pre{white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-wrap:anywhere}@media(max-width:700px){main{padding:9px}}@media print{body{background:white}.fig,.fig img{background:white!important}}.remember{background:#ecf8ef;border-left:5px solid #2b8550;padding:12px;border-radius:8px;margin-top:12px}.source-links{font-size:13px;text-align:left;overflow-wrap:anywhere}.source-links a{word-break:break-all}.lesson h4{color:#29425a;margin-bottom:4px}.lesson p{margin-top:4px}</style></head><body><main><section class="hero"><h1>Daily Multivendor Routing Lab</h1><p><b>Run timestamp:</b> 2026-08-29-08-11</p><p>Cisco • Fortinet • Palo Alto Networks • Arista • Aruba/HPE • Juniper • FRRouting</p></section><section class="card"><h2>Executive dashboard</h2><p><b>20 detailed lessons</b> across seven vendor groups. Core model: adjacency → protocol DB → RIB → recursion → FIB/hardware → overlay/multichassis → service health.</p><p><b>Exam:</b> exactly 50 questions; 20 current/recent and 30 cumulative; 80% pass threshold.</p></section><h2>Detailed source lessons</h2><article class="lesson"><span class="badge">Cisco</span><h3>1. Hierarchical eBGP EVPN multihoming</h3><p><b>Source:</b> <a href="https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/td/docs/switches/lan/catalyst_standalones/Multihoming/multihoming-in-bgp-evpn-vxlan-fabric/hierarchical-multihoming/configure-ebgp-based-evpn-multihoming-and-fabric-network.html">https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/td/docs/switches/lan/catalyst_standalones/Multihoming/multihoming-in-bgp-evpn-vxlan-fabric/hierarchical-multihoming/configure-ebgp-based-evpn-multihoming-and-fabric-network.html</a></p><h4>Architecture and intent</h4><p>Cisco’s hierarchical eBGP EVPN design separates distribution/leaf routing domains from the spine/border layer so a large fabric can scale without forcing every leaf into one flat BGP topology. The spine behaves as a control-plane transit point: it learns EVPN reachability from one block and distributes it to another while policy preserves the originating VTEP as the BGP next hop. The design goal is that the spine does not accidentally become a VXLAN decapsulation/recapsulation waypoint for ordinary east-west traffic.</p><h4>Control-plane sequence</h4><p>A leaf originates EVPN NLRI for tenant MAC/IP or prefix reachability. The spine receives the route through eBGP, applies the hierarchical policy, and advertises it onward without changing the VTEP next hop. The receiving leaf imports the route according to RD/RT policy, then performs recursive resolution of the advertised next hop through the underlay. Only after that recursive resolution succeeds can the route be considered usable for forwarding.</p><h4>Why routes can look healthy while traffic fails</h4><p>BGP session state and EVPN route presence prove only part of the path. If the retained remote VTEP address is missing from the underlay RIB, resolves through the wrong VRF, or is not programmed into the FIB, the EVPN route can remain visible but unusable. This distinction is especially important in multivendor fabrics because operators often focus on route-target import and overlook the simpler underlay recursion failure.</p><h4>Verification workflow</h4><p>Verify the hierarchy in order: eBGP adjacency, received EVPN NLRI, advertised next-hop value, underlay route to that VTEP, FIB/adjacency programming, then VXLAN encapsulation and tenant forwarding. If the first four states are correct but packets still fail, move to VNI/VRF mapping, MTU, BUM handling, and multihoming state instead of continuing to tune BGP policy.</p><h4>Interoperability and design guidance</h4><p>Arista, Juniper, FRR and Aruba/HPE all implement EVPN/VXLAN with similar underlay/overlay separation, but their route-server, next-hop, and multichassis defaults can differ. Document whether each eBGP boundary rewrites next hop, which address family carries VTEP reachability, and which loopback is used as the tunnel source. Never assume a vendor’s default next-hop behavior matches Cisco’s hierarchical reference design.</p><details open=""><summary>Verification commands / evidence</summary><pre>show bgp l2vpn evpn summary
show bgp l2vpn evpn
show ip route &lt;remote-vtep&gt;</pre></details><p><b>Reproducible lab: </b>Build the smallest reproducible lab for lesson 1, establish a known-good baseline, break exactly one dependency described above, capture the first changed control-plane and data-plane evidence, remediate it, and verify recovery without changing unrelated features.</p><div class="remember">Active recall: identify the first failing state boundary, the exact evidence that proves it, and the smallest correction that restores the intended design.</div></article><article class="lesson"><span class="badge">Cisco</span><h3>2. All-active EVPN multihoming consistency</h3><p><b>Source:</b> <a href="https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/td/docs/switches/lan/catalyst_standalones/Multihoming/multihoming-in-bgp-evpn-vxlan-fabric/all-active-mode/restriction-for-aa-multihoming.html">https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/td/docs/switches/lan/catalyst_standalones/Multihoming/multihoming-in-bgp-evpn-vxlan-fabric/all-active-mode/restriction-for-aa-multihoming.html</a></p><h4>What Cisco is warning about</h4><p>Cisco’s current all-active EVPN multihoming restrictions are operationally important because an active/active Ethernet Segment is only safe when both VTEPs advertise a consistent representation of the service. The pair must agree on the Ethernet Segment, service/VNI state, and relevant overlay attributes. A mismatch can create a partial control plane in which BGP sessions remain up but remote devices receive an incomplete or contradictory view of the multihomed attachment.</p><h4>Failure mechanism</h4><p>Remote VTEPs use EVPN route types and Ethernet Segment information to determine reachability, aliasing, and designated-forwarder behavior. If one local VTEP is missing the EVPN instance, VLAN/VNI mapping, or Ethernet Segment state, some destinations can be attracted to a device that cannot forward them. That produces blackholing that is easy to misread as a generic BGP or LACP problem.</p><h4>Platform/release caveats</h4><p>Cisco also documents unsupported combinations and platform restrictions in IOS XE 26.x, including selected Catalyst families, certain combinations with StackWise Virtual, IPv6-only underlay for EVPN multihoming, Layer-2 leaf use in centralized-gateway designs, FHRP on anycast-gateway SVIs, REP/PVLAN on Ethernet Segment ports, and selected access-security features. Those restrictions should be treated as design constraints, not merely troubleshooting footnotes.</p><h4>Troubleshooting method</h4><p>Compare both VTEPs side by side. Verify Ethernet Segment identity, EVPN instance/VNI mapping, port-channel/LACP state, local VLAN membership, EVPN advertisements, remote next-hop reachability, and the forwarding result from both chassis. A single-node inspection is insufficient in an active/active design because the defect may be asymmetry between peers.</p><h4>Prevention</h4><p>Use a pair-consistency checklist in provisioning and upgrades. Validate both the configured state and the operational state before introducing endpoints, and repeat the comparison after software upgrades or service changes. This practice generalizes directly to Arista MLAG/EVPN and Aruba VSX/EVPN even though the proprietary synchronization mechanisms differ.</p><details open=""><summary>Verification commands / evidence</summary><pre>show bgp l2vpn evpn
show l2vpn evpn ethernet-segment
show etherchannel summary</pre></details><p><b>Reproducible lab: </b>Build the smallest reproducible lab for lesson 2, establish a known-good baseline, break exactly one dependency described above, capture the first changed control-plane and data-plane evidence, remediate it, and verify recovery without changing unrelated features.</p><div class="remember">Active recall: identify the first failing state boundary, the exact evidence that proves it, and the smallest correction that restores the intended design.</div></article><article class="lesson"><span class="badge">Cisco</span><h3>3. Routed overlay and IRB mapping boundaries</h3><p><b>Source:</b> <a href="https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/td/docs/switches/lan/catalyst_standalones/Multihoming/multihoming-in-bgp-evpn-vxlan-fabric/routed-overlay-mh/routed-overlay-network-reference-configuration.html">https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/td/docs/switches/lan/catalyst_standalones/Multihoming/multihoming-in-bgp-evpn-vxlan-fabric/routed-overlay-mh/routed-overlay-network-reference-configuration.html</a></p><h4>Routed-overlay mental model</h4><p>The routed-overlay reference design combines host-facing redundancy, a Layer-3 underlay, and EVPN/VXLAN tenant services. Those layers must be tested independently. A healthy access EtherChannel or Ethernet Segment proves only that the endpoint has redundant attachment; it does not prove tenant route import, remote VTEP recursion, or overlay forwarding.</p><h4>Mapping boundaries that commonly fail</h4><p>Treat VLAN↔EVI/VNI, SVI↔VRF, VRF↔route-target, EVPN route↔next-hop, and endpoint MAC/IP↔local attachment as explicit state boundaries. A single mismatch can produce a fabric that is almost correct: routes may exist, tunnels may be up, and the local gateway may respond, while one tenant subnet still blackholes.</p><h4>Control/data-plane sequence</h4><p>A host resolves its first-hop gateway locally. The VTEP performs bridging or IRB according to the destination, selects tenant reachability from the VRF, resolves the remote VTEP in the underlay, encapsulates into VXLAN, and sends the outer IP packet across the fabric. The receiving VTEP decapsulates and performs local bridging/routing. Every step is independently verifiable.</p><h4>Troubleshooting</h4><p>Start at the endpoint and stop at the first missing state: local VLAN and gateway/ARP, local VRF route, EVPN route import, underlay VTEP recursion, VXLAN encapsulation, remote endpoint learning. This method prevents the common habit of changing BGP policy when the real failure is a local VLAN/VNI mismatch.</p><h4>Design guidance</h4><p>Stretch only the services that require it, keep tenant and underlay routing tables clearly separated, and document each mapping. In multivendor environments, normalize terminology—VNI, EVI, bridge-domain, routing-instance/VRF—so the engineering team compares equivalent objects rather than command names.</p><details open=""><summary>Verification commands / evidence</summary><pre>show bgp l2vpn evpn
show ip route vrf &lt;vrf&gt; &lt;prefix&gt;
show ip route &lt;remote-vtep&gt;</pre></details><p><b>Reproducible lab: </b>Build the 